WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our next guest is an
Academy Award and Grammy winner,

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.137
a Rock &amp; Roll Hall of Fame
inductee,

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:07.307
and former front man
of Talking Heads.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.642
His Tony Award-winning
Broadway show,

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.810
"American Utopia," is
running through April

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.212
at the St. James Theatre.

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:15.781
Please welcome back to the show
our friend David Byrne.

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:17.717
Hello, David. How are you?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.819
-Hi, Seth. Hi, Seth.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.053
Great to be back.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:22.255
-It's great to have you back.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.790
A surreal time to be doing
a Broadway show

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:28.394
these last two years,
especially these last few weeks.

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:30.763
I know that you had an outbreak
at the show.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:32.631
You did not have to cancel it,
though.

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:34.501
You did sort of
a modified performance.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.537
How did that come about?
-Wow, yes, yes.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:41.874
Like a lot of shows, we had,
like, a couple of people

00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:43.909
who tested positive
and had to go out,

00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:45.611
and they were covered
by understudies.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.981
And then more,
and then more, and then more.

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:51.150
until half the cast,
half the band was out,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.552
and some of the crew, as well,

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.621
and we couldn't do the show
anymore.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.790
And some of us talked
amongst ourselves

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.257
and said, "Well, maybe we can do

00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:00.626
a kind of unplugged
kind of thing.

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:03.863
We'll do some other songs,
some, you know, catalog songs,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.931
other, you know, Talking Heads
songs, whatever,

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:09.201
and just kind of see if the
audience wants to hear that.

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:11.704
We won't be doing
any of the choreography,

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:14.207
we won't be doing this show,
but we'll give them something.

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:15.675
So, if they come
from out of town,

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:20.313
as people did over the holidays,
we'll have something for them."

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:24.050
And they can -- as I said
in this little message --

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.519
I said, "You can either
get your money back

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.321
or you can take
what's behind the curtain."

00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.356
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.359
-I would imagine people
found that exciting,

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.827
to be able to go to a show

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:38.664
and get something more unique
than what the normal show was.

00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:40.833
-Yeah, I think
audiences really liked it.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:44.269
It was not slick and perfect.

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:47.974
There were times when I had to
stop and ask the band,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.076
"Who starts this song?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.212
And the audience loved that.

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:55.448
They would, like,
applaud for stuff like that.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.716
-Your show, which I saw
before the pandemic

00:01:57.716 --> 00:02:00.987
and loved a great deal,
it's very optimistic.

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:03.456
it also has
a lot of audience engagement.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.691
You ask them
to get up and dance.

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:08.094
Are people still
engaged with the show?

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:09.863
Are people still happy
to get up and dance

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.598
when you demand it of them?

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.732
-They are getting up and dancing

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:16.369
even to this kind of unplugged
version that we're doing.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.739
So, yeah, says something
about audiences

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.573
that they really
want to be together.

00:02:21.573 --> 00:02:23.876
They really want to kind of
express themselves

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.711
and dance and have a good time.

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:28.881
And a shout-out to Gustavo
over there on the bandstand.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:30.983
-Yes, we're very happy.
Thank you so much.

00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:34.153
-Hey!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:35.788
-Thank you for
lending us your drummer.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:38.658
One of the -- You know,
the show looks beautiful,

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.327
and one of the things about it

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:44.263
is, the, you know -- the
wardrobe is gray suits.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.032
Also, you're barefoot.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:49.469
How did you come up with that
decision of those two elements

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.305
of how the show would look?

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.440
-Uh, little by little.
It was very practical.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.276
I thought suits would kind of

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:59.379
make everyone look
sort of attractive

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:02.781
and kind of, well,
almost like one size fits all.

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:06.352
You can kind of -- everybody can
look good, decent in a suit,

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:07.853
women and men.

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:11.824
And then I -- And just asked
the lighting director.

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.192
I said, "What color
do you want them?"

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:17.964
And they said, "Medium gray."
immediately, medium gray.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:19.232
-[ Laughs ]
-I thought, "Okay."

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.468
That was so that when we --
if they put light on us,

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.237
we can be seen;

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:27.540
and if they take the light away,
we can disappear.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.843
if we go kind of
upstage into the darkness,

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:32.311
we'll disappear.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:35.882
If we were wearing a white suit,
which I love to wear sometimes,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:39.818
it would be really hard
to have me kind of disappear.

00:03:39.818 --> 00:03:41.920
So it's purely practical.
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.057
-And the bare feet?
Um, the bare feet --

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:47.727
I mean, once you're
in a business suit,

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.561
I thought, "We're not gonna wear
business shoes.

00:03:49.561 --> 00:03:52.665
That's going a little too far."

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:53.866
-Yeah.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.935
You know, you're still
a rock 'n' roller.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.703
You can't be wearing
a suit and shoes.

00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.405
-[ Laughing ] Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:04.077
By the way, I was thinking
about the Florida man.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:05.945
-Yeah.
-And I remembered

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:12.485
that when they apprehended him,
they asked him, "What is this?"

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.554
And he said, "It's not mine."

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:17.824
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:23.129
That's -- I don't know where the
story went after that, but...

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.164
[ Laughter ]
-I like that.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.166
Yeah, that's true.
I mean, I feel like

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:31.204
that's every drug user's
first response to the cops,

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:33.806
is, "It's not mine."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.909
You -- there's a wonderful film
of the concert --

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.378
of the show, I should say --
on HBO Max

00:04:39.378 --> 00:04:41.714
that Spike Lee directed.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:45.317
Obviously, both of you have had
a long history in New York City.

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:47.787
When did you first meet?

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.889
-I think -- I'm not sure.
I think it might have been

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:54.260
when he did
"Do the Right Thing".

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.095
I remember going
to the premiere of that.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:58.564
It was at the Ziegfeld.
It was like, "Wow.

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:03.703
This up-and-coming filmmaker
now has a film at the Ziegfeld."

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.372
And it's an amazing film,
if people haven't seen it.

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:09.708
I might have met him then
or some other time.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:12.845
We started crossing paths.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.247
We were both kind of coming up
at the same time.

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:20.119
-I think a lot of people sort of
romanticize 1970s New York,

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:21.453
which is when
you first came here.

00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:24.824
But can you tell those of us
who are so upset

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.859
that we didn't get to live
in New York City then

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:29.729
at least one thing about it
that was worse?

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.030
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.664
-I can tell you a lot of things
that are worse.

00:05:32.664 --> 00:05:36.769
There was -- There was this law

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:39.405
that got passed in New York
sometime after that.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.441
It was called
the pooper scooper law.

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:45.844
And for those people who don't
remember or weren't around,

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:50.149
they used to let dogs, well,
poop right on the sidewalk,

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:52.651
and the owner
would just walk on.

00:05:52.651 --> 00:05:55.788
Like, uh, "Okay, that's --

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:58.090
You've done your business.
We're just moving on."

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.691
So if you were walking around
in New York,

00:05:59.691 --> 00:06:02.094
you had to be looking down
all the time.

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.429
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:05.531
-And inevitably, even though

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.165
you're trying
to be really careful,

00:06:07.165 --> 00:06:08.634
you'd get some on you.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.871
And you'd be there on the curb
or on the grass or somewhere,

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:14.006
trying to scrape it
off your shoes.

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:17.376
And that's not romantic.
There's nothing --

00:06:17.376 --> 00:06:19.978
There's nothing good about that.
-No.

00:06:19.978 --> 00:06:22.248
And then, if somebody walked by
while you were doing it,

00:06:22.248 --> 00:06:24.216
you would say, "It's not mine,"
right?

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:25.918
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.320
"I don't know
who put that there."

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.289
[ Laughter ]
-Hey, you also --

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.925
You have a book of your artwork
coming out soon.

00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:36.929
This is a beautiful drawing.
Is this all art you did

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:40.566
during the pandemic,
or is this sort of predating?

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:43.202
-It's all during the pandemic.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:47.640
As I think happened
to a lot of people

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.309
during the pandemic,
we were sometimes prohibited

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.945
from doing the things
that we normally do --

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:56.281
either going to work
or doing concerts

00:06:56.281 --> 00:06:57.517
or whatever it might be.

00:06:57.517 --> 00:07:01.553
And some of us took up cooking
or making bread,

00:07:01.553 --> 00:07:03.422
and I started drawing.

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:05.824
And to some extent,
I think the drawings

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:10.495
kind of reflected the turmoil
that was going through --

00:07:10.495 --> 00:07:12.564
going through not just my head,

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:15.434
but, I assume,
other people's heads, too.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:16.669
-Well, it's really beautiful,

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.270
and I look forward
to seeing the book.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.838
And I also look forward

00:07:19.838 --> 00:07:21.140
to coming back
and seeing the show again.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.741
Thank you so much
for being here, David.

00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:24.877
-Thank you.
Good to see you again.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.145
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.347
-David Byrne, everybody.
"American Utopia"

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.248
is running through April
at the St. James Theatre

00:07:30.248 --> 00:07:31.551
and streaming on HBO Max.

